Subject: [PATCH 70/70] arch/unix: point the hosted load-base at mapped memory -- `load` on openbios-unix no longer segfaults on the default

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc unix

THE HOSTED DOOR'S DEFAULT load-base WAS UNMAPPED. forth/admin/nvram.fs defaults
load-base to 0x4000000 (64 MiB). On bare-metal amd64 that is real RAM and
correct; but openbios-unix is a HOSTED process whose memory is three mmap'd
regions (the Forth arena at 0x20000000, the dictionary at 0x30000000, and
below-4G scratch), and nothing is mapped at 0x4000000. So `load <dev>:\FILE` read
the file straight into unmapped memory and the engine took

    panic: segmentation violation at 4000000

inside the device read -- every time, unless a track `$setenv`'d load-base into
the arena first. Every unix load track (elf-ladder, the dsl showcases) carried
that workaround. Measured while building B.4 Spike 0, parked in TODO B.4 as
finding (b).

THE FIX mirrors x86. arch/x86/openbios.c already feval's a load-base that is
actually reachable (there because only C knows virt_offset after the GDT
rebase). Here only C knows the host mapping, so arch_init() mmaps a dedicated
16 MiB load area below 4 GiB and feval's `<addr> constant load-base`. It is a
SEPARATE region, not the Forth arena: load-base inside the alloc-mem heap would
be clobbered by the next allocation. The constant shadows the nvram config word
(a redefinition, exactly as x86 does), which stays put so `" load-base"
evaluate` still resolves if this ever regresses.

SCOPE. The change is in arch/unix/unix.c, which is compiled into NOTHING but
openbios-unix -- x86, amd64 and ppc do not build it, so their images are
byte-unaffected by construction (not merely "retested green"). The `unix` leg is
what this patch validates.

VERIFIED, control run not reasoned. `./smoke-openbios.sh hosted-load`:
  - WITHOUT the fix (reverted, rebuilt): load-base reads 4000000 and
    `load hd:\PAYLOAD.BIN` panics "segmentation violation at 4000000".
  - WITH the fix: load-base reads a mapped address, the 200 KiB file lands there
    (load-size correct, load-base c@ == the file's first byte), no panic.
The track asserts both that the default is no longer the unmapped 0x4000000 AND
that the load lands -- a regression to the old default resurfaces as the segfault.

Verified by: ./smoke-openbios.sh hosted-load

--- a/arch/unix/unix.c
+++ b/arch/unix/unix.c
@@ -51,6 +51,16 @@
  * into the mapping's neighbour. The dictionary is one mmap below 4 GiB at
  * 0x30000000; the 4 MiB Forth arena is at 0x20000000, so nothing moves. */
 #define DICTIONARY_SIZE	(1024*1024)	/* 1 MiB for the dictionary  */
+/* mklab (TODO B.4 finding b): a mapped load area for the hosted door's
+ * load-base. The nvram default (0x4000000) is a bare physical address that is
+ * mapped to NOTHING in this host process, so `load <file>` read the file into
+ * unmapped memory and the engine took "panic: segmentation violation" inside
+ * the device read -- every time, unless a track $setenv'd load-base into the
+ * arena first. 16 MiB is generous: the largest image the lab loads here is the
+ * ~1.16 MiB firmware ELF, and a hosted process has address space to spare.
+ * SEPARATE from the Forth arena on purpose -- load-base inside the alloc-mem
+ * heap would be clobbered by the next allocation. */
+#define LOAD_AREA_SIZE	(16*1024*1024)
 
 #if defined(_FILE_OFFSET_BITS) && (_FILE_OFFSET_BITS==64)
 #define lseek lseek64
@@ -636,6 +646,26 @@
          * gate in libopenbios/elf_load.c refuses the copy by name and leaves
          * the image at load-base, where the toolkit can still read it. */
         elf_segments_placeable = 0;
+
+        /* mklab (TODO B.4 finding b): point load-base at a mapped buffer.
+         * forth/admin/nvram.fs defaults it to 0x4000000 -- correct on bare-metal
+         * amd64 (real RAM), WRONG in this host process, where nothing is mapped
+         * there and `load` segfaulted in the device read. x86 solves the same
+         * shape by feval'ing a mapped load-base (arch/x86/openbios.c); only C
+         * knows the host mapping, so do it here. This shadows the nvram config
+         * word (a redefinition, like x86's), which stays put so `" load-base"
+         * evaluate` still resolves if this ever regresses. */
+        {
+                void *lb = alloc_below_4g(LOAD_AREA_SIZE, 0x10000000,
+                                          "the hosted load area");
+                if (lb) {
+                        char buf[64];
+                        /* Forth's base is hex here, matching nvram.fs. */
+                        snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), "%lx constant load-base",
+                                 (unsigned long)(uintptr_t)lb);
+                        feval(buf);
+                }
+        }
 }
 
 int
